1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,897
Two things about the Czinger 21C that

2
00:00:02,897 --> 00:00:04,934
most car fans do not know.

3
00:00:05,114 --> 00:00:07,978
First, much of this hypercar was designed

4
00:00:07,978 --> 00:00:10,004
to be made without the molds,

5
00:00:10,004 --> 00:00:12,729
dies and fixtures that normally shape a

6
00:00:12,729 --> 00:00:13,009
car.

7
00:00:13,189 --> 00:00:15,885
Second, the same car has taken the

8
00:00:15,885 --> 00:00:19,851
Laguna Seca production-car lap record three times,

9
00:00:19,851 --> 00:00:22,151
by a bigger margin each time.

10
00:00:22,331 --> 00:00:25,025
We checked both claims against the sources.

11
00:00:25,375 --> 00:00:29,151
Our sources are Wikipedia's page on the

12
00:00:29,151 --> 00:00:33,509
21C, an engineering article from the American

13
00:00:33,509 --> 00:00:36,608
Society of Mechanical Engineers,

14
00:00:36,608 --> 00:00:38,835
a Backfire News report,

15
00:00:38,835 --> 00:00:44,162
GTPlanet, FastCar, Facfox and a Caltech-linked business

16
00:00:44,162 --> 00:00:46,098
magazine called CSQ.

17
00:00:46,278 --> 00:00:47,761
Where they disagree,

18
00:00:47,761 --> 00:00:49,540
and they disagree a lot,

19
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:51,096
we show both numbers.

20
00:00:51,276 --> 00:00:54,272
Our own arithmetic is always labelled.

21
00:00:54,621 --> 00:00:56,874
Start with what the car is.

22
00:00:57,054 --> 00:00:59,933
Wikipedia says the 21C is made by

23
00:00:59,933 --> 00:01:02,725
Czinger Vehicles in Los Angeles,

24
00:01:02,725 --> 00:01:05,692
with a planned run of eighty cars.

25
00:01:05,872 --> 00:01:07,987
It has a two point eight eight

26
00:01:07,987 --> 00:01:09,397
litre twin-turbo V8,

27
00:01:09,397 --> 00:01:12,570
two electric motors driving the front wheels,

28
00:01:12,570 --> 00:01:15,319
and a combined output of twelve hundred

29
00:01:15,319 --> 00:01:16,518
fifty horsepower.

30
00:01:16,698 --> 00:01:17,952
That is the headline.

31
00:01:18,132 --> 00:01:20,085
The unusual part is how it is

32
00:01:20,085 --> 00:01:20,489
built.

33
00:01:20,839 --> 00:01:24,937
The American Society of Mechanical Engineers described

34
00:01:24,937 --> 00:01:28,048
the approach in March twenty twenty-five.

35
00:01:28,228 --> 00:01:31,664
Its article says Czinger's parts are designed

36
00:01:31,664 --> 00:01:35,557
by a computer process called topology optimization,

37
00:01:35,557 --> 00:01:38,535
which decides where material should be,

38
00:01:38,535 --> 00:01:41,054
based on the stresses a part will

39
00:01:41,054 --> 00:01:41,359
see.

40
00:01:41,539 --> 00:01:44,729
The article says this frees engineers from

41
00:01:44,729 --> 00:01:46,855
limits such as draft angles,

42
00:01:46,855 --> 00:01:49,817
minimum wall thickness and tool access.

43
00:01:50,167 --> 00:01:51,687
In a normal factory,

44
00:01:51,687 --> 00:01:53,587
a cast part needs a mold,

45
00:01:53,587 --> 00:01:56,019
and a stamped panel needs a die.

46
00:01:56,199 --> 00:01:58,947
Those cost money and time before the

47
00:01:58,947 --> 00:02:00,320
first part exists.

48
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:03,435
The ASME article says that with printed

49
00:02:03,435 --> 00:02:05,617
parts, a one-off can be made,

50
00:02:05,617 --> 00:02:09,604
instrumented, tested and improved without waiting for

51
00:02:09,604 --> 00:02:10,206
tooling.

52
00:02:10,386 --> 00:02:12,747
Field data can then feed back into

53
00:02:12,747 --> 00:02:13,718
the optimizer.

54
00:02:13,898 --> 00:02:15,797
That is the claim in the article,

55
00:02:15,797 --> 00:02:17,695
not a measurement we could check.

56
00:02:18,044 --> 00:02:19,867
Three parts show it best.

57
00:02:20,047 --> 00:02:22,462
The first is the gearbox case.

58
00:02:22,642 --> 00:02:26,067
ASME calls it the first topology-optimized,

59
00:02:26,067 --> 00:02:30,448
additively manufactured production gearbox case to move

60
00:02:30,448 --> 00:02:32,599
beyond the prototype stage,

61
00:02:32,599 --> 00:02:35,785
made from a proprietary aluminium alloy.

62
00:02:35,965 --> 00:02:38,663
The article says the design went through

63
00:02:38,663 --> 00:02:42,239
multiple iterations, and that heat exchanger elements

64
00:02:42,239 --> 00:02:44,196
are built into the structure.

65
00:02:44,546 --> 00:02:46,959
The second is the rear subframe,

66
00:02:46,959 --> 00:02:49,748
which ASME says is the largest single

67
00:02:49,748 --> 00:02:50,502
component.

68
00:02:50,682 --> 00:02:52,628
It is printed in sections,

69
00:02:52,628 --> 00:02:54,498
then laser-metal printed,

70
00:02:54,498 --> 00:02:57,341
then assembled in a robotic cell using

71
00:02:57,341 --> 00:02:58,763
in-house adhesives.

72
00:02:58,943 --> 00:03:02,381
The article describes the cell as fixtureless:

73
00:03:02,381 --> 00:03:06,717
robots place parts using laser-tracked positioning instead

74
00:03:06,717 --> 00:03:08,660
of clamping them in a jig.

75
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:11,148
The article does not give the number

76
00:03:11,148 --> 00:03:11,917
of sections.

77
00:03:12,267 --> 00:03:14,322
The third is a part called the

78
00:03:14,322 --> 00:03:15,007
BrakeNode.

79
00:03:15,187 --> 00:03:18,266
Wikipedia says it combines the brake caliper

80
00:03:18,266 --> 00:03:20,365
and the upright into one unit.

81
00:03:20,545 --> 00:03:23,161
ASME adds that it also carries the

82
00:03:23,161 --> 00:03:25,546
brake fluid, lowers part count,

83
00:03:25,546 --> 00:03:28,777
simplifies assembly and improves rigidity.

84
00:03:28,957 --> 00:03:30,350
In an ordinary car,

85
00:03:30,350 --> 00:03:32,770
a caliper bolts to a separate hub

86
00:03:32,770 --> 00:03:33,357
carrier.

87
00:03:33,537 --> 00:03:35,697
Here they are a single printed structure.

88
00:03:36,046 --> 00:03:38,451
Printing metal at this scale is hard,

89
00:03:38,451 --> 00:03:40,075
and the sources admit it.

90
00:03:40,255 --> 00:03:43,752
ASME says early challenges included porosity,

91
00:03:43,752 --> 00:03:46,550
meaning tiny voids inside the metal,

92
00:03:46,550 --> 00:03:48,881
and print-orientation effects,

93
00:03:48,881 --> 00:03:51,756
where strength depends on which way a

94
00:03:51,756 --> 00:03:52,922
part was built.

95
00:03:53,102 --> 00:03:55,958
The article quotes Chief Engineer Ewan Baldry

96
00:03:55,958 --> 00:03:58,815
saying these have since been resolved through

97
00:03:58,815 --> 00:04:00,021
process refinement.

98
00:04:00,201 --> 00:04:02,248
We could not find test data in

99
00:04:02,248 --> 00:04:02,999
any source.

100
00:04:03,349 --> 00:04:04,859
The printers matter too.

101
00:04:05,039 --> 00:04:09,272
Facfox reports that Czinger's sister company Divergent

102
00:04:09,272 --> 00:04:13,191
bought five pre-production metal printers from SLM

103
00:04:13,191 --> 00:04:15,465
Solutions in twenty nineteen.

104
00:04:15,645 --> 00:04:18,354
It says the suspension and chassis parts

105
00:04:18,354 --> 00:04:21,401
were made on commercially available SLM eight

106
00:04:21,401 --> 00:04:24,110
hundred machines, with a build volume of

107
00:04:24,110 --> 00:04:26,616
five hundred by two hundred eighty by

108
00:04:26,616 --> 00:04:28,783
eight hundred fifty millimetres,

109
00:04:28,783 --> 00:04:32,711
and that Divergent planned twenty next-generation systems.

110
00:04:33,061 --> 00:04:34,271
By our arithmetic,

111
00:04:34,271 --> 00:04:36,556
a part that fits that build volume

112
00:04:36,556 --> 00:04:38,975
could be at most about eight hundred

113
00:04:38,975 --> 00:04:40,521
fifty millimetres tall,

114
00:04:40,521 --> 00:04:42,268
which is under three feet.

115
00:04:42,448 --> 00:04:44,446
That is why the biggest parts,

116
00:04:44,446 --> 00:04:45,644
like the subframe,

117
00:04:45,644 --> 00:04:47,975
are printed in sections and joined.

118
00:04:48,155 --> 00:04:50,595
That is our inference from the stated

119
00:04:50,595 --> 00:04:52,903
size, not a statement from Czinger.

120
00:04:53,253 --> 00:04:56,021
The Backfire News report adds a number

121
00:04:56,021 --> 00:04:56,968
about labour.

122
00:04:57,148 --> 00:05:00,092
It says each 21C takes three thousand

123
00:05:00,092 --> 00:05:02,558
to four thousand hours of work.

124
00:05:02,738 --> 00:05:04,111
By our arithmetic,

125
00:05:04,111 --> 00:05:05,790
at forty hours a week,

126
00:05:05,790 --> 00:05:08,919
that is seventy-five to one hundred weeks

127
00:05:08,919 --> 00:05:10,063
for one person,

128
00:05:10,063 --> 00:05:12,200
so roughly a year and a half

129
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:14,336
to nearly two years per car.

130
00:05:14,516 --> 00:05:15,969
A toolless car,

131
00:05:15,969 --> 00:05:18,486
then, is not a fast car to

132
00:05:18,486 --> 00:05:19,067
build.

133
00:05:19,418 --> 00:05:21,240
One more claim needs care.

134
00:05:21,420 --> 00:05:24,266
GTPlanet, in February twenty twenty,

135
00:05:24,266 --> 00:05:27,507
says the car is made from three-D-printed

136
00:05:27,507 --> 00:05:28,851
carbon nanotubes.

137
00:05:29,031 --> 00:05:31,387
No other source we read says that.

138
00:05:31,567 --> 00:05:35,459
ASME describes aluminium alloy printed parts and

139
00:05:35,459 --> 00:05:40,080
adhesive-bonded structure, and Wikipedia does not mention

140
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:40,890
nanotubes.

141
00:05:41,070 --> 00:05:43,464
We think the GTPlanet line is an

142
00:05:43,464 --> 00:05:45,708
error, and we do not repeat it

143
00:05:45,708 --> 00:05:46,306
as fact.

144
00:05:46,657 --> 00:05:49,861
The company also makes an environmental claim.

145
00:05:50,041 --> 00:05:52,702
The CSQ article says the 21C is

146
00:05:52,702 --> 00:05:56,908
net-zero in emissions when run on carbon-recycled

147
00:05:56,908 --> 00:05:57,680
methanol.

148
00:05:57,860 --> 00:06:00,021
That is a claim about a fuel,

149
00:06:00,021 --> 00:06:02,331
not about the car as delivered,

150
00:06:02,331 --> 00:06:05,014
and the article does not explain who

151
00:06:05,014 --> 00:06:07,994
supplies that methanol or how the carbon

152
00:06:07,994 --> 00:06:09,261
is accounted for.

153
00:06:09,441 --> 00:06:11,055
We list it as a claim.

154
00:06:11,404 --> 00:06:13,258
Even the factory is part of the

155
00:06:13,258 --> 00:06:13,796
argument.

156
00:06:13,976 --> 00:06:17,293
CSQ says Czinger Vehicles had fewer than

157
00:06:17,293 --> 00:06:20,362
fifty employees in twenty twenty-two.

158
00:06:20,542 --> 00:06:23,500
The ASME article says McLaren and Bugatti

159
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:26,676
have begun working with Divergent on printed

160
00:06:26,676 --> 00:06:27,902
structural parts.

161
00:06:28,082 --> 00:06:31,041
So the toolless method is being offered

162
00:06:31,041 --> 00:06:32,483
to other carmakers.

163
00:06:32,663 --> 00:06:34,389
How many parts they use,

164
00:06:34,389 --> 00:06:35,684
and on which cars,

165
00:06:35,684 --> 00:06:36,691
is not stated.

166
00:06:37,041 --> 00:06:38,930
Before the second thing,

167
00:06:38,930 --> 00:06:41,054
a small one that is easy to

168
00:06:41,054 --> 00:06:41,604
verify.

169
00:06:41,784 --> 00:06:44,844
Wikipedia says the 21C seats the driver

170
00:06:44,844 --> 00:06:45,943
in the centre,

171
00:06:45,943 --> 00:06:48,924
with a passenger seat directly behind.

172
00:06:49,104 --> 00:06:52,197
FastCar says the driver sits centrally like

173
00:06:52,197 --> 00:06:53,491
in the McLaren F1,

174
00:06:53,491 --> 00:06:56,512
and quotes Czinger that this gives optimal

175
00:06:56,512 --> 00:06:59,101
performance and weight distribution.

176
00:06:59,281 --> 00:07:00,548
Czinger says it,

177
00:07:00,548 --> 00:07:01,974
we cannot test it.

178
00:07:02,323 --> 00:07:04,772
Wikipedia adds a practical reason:

179
00:07:04,772 --> 00:07:07,293
the layout minimises cockpit width.

180
00:07:07,473 --> 00:07:10,298
The car's overall width is two thousand

181
00:07:10,298 --> 00:07:13,630
fifty millimetres, which is eighty point seven

182
00:07:13,630 --> 00:07:14,137
inches.

183
00:07:14,317 --> 00:07:15,762
By our arithmetic,

184
00:07:15,762 --> 00:07:18,493
that is about six feet nine inches

185
00:07:18,493 --> 00:07:20,983
across, wide for a car with one

186
00:07:20,983 --> 00:07:22,107
seat in front.

187
00:07:22,287 --> 00:07:24,591
The sources do not give the cabin

188
00:07:24,591 --> 00:07:26,824
width, so we cannot say how much

189
00:07:26,824 --> 00:07:27,871
narrower it is.

190
00:07:28,221 --> 00:07:29,754
Now the second thing.

191
00:07:29,934 --> 00:07:33,809
Wikipedia lists three Laguna Seca lap records

192
00:07:33,809 --> 00:07:34,842
for the 21C,

193
00:07:34,842 --> 00:07:38,200
all set by American driver Joel Miller.

194
00:07:38,380 --> 00:07:40,063
August twenty twenty-one,

195
00:07:40,063 --> 00:07:42,688
one minute twenty-five point four four.

196
00:07:42,868 --> 00:07:44,638
August twenty twenty-four,

197
00:07:44,638 --> 00:07:47,361
one minute twenty-four point seven five.

198
00:07:47,541 --> 00:07:50,741
And the fifteenth of December twenty twenty-five,

199
00:07:50,741 --> 00:07:53,288
one minute twenty-two point three zero.

200
00:07:53,638 --> 00:07:56,921
Wikipedia says the first two used road-legal

201
00:07:56,921 --> 00:07:59,607
Michelin Pilot Sport Cup two R tyres

202
00:07:59,607 --> 00:08:02,592
on a high-downforce track version of the

203
00:08:02,592 --> 00:08:02,891
car.

204
00:08:03,071 --> 00:08:04,881
It does not name the tyres for

205
00:08:04,881 --> 00:08:07,053
the December twenty twenty-five lap.

206
00:08:07,233 --> 00:08:09,216
So we cannot say how much of

207
00:08:09,216 --> 00:08:12,050
that last improvement came from the car,

208
00:08:12,050 --> 00:08:14,884
the tyres, the driver or the conditions.

209
00:08:15,064 --> 00:08:16,515
We flag that gap.

210
00:08:16,865 --> 00:08:18,136
By our arithmetic,

211
00:08:18,136 --> 00:08:21,170
the August twenty twenty-four lap was point

212
00:08:21,170 --> 00:08:23,992
six nine seconds quicker than the twenty

213
00:08:23,992 --> 00:08:25,051
twenty-one one.

214
00:08:25,231 --> 00:08:28,267
The December twenty twenty-five lap was another

215
00:08:28,267 --> 00:08:30,852
two point four five seconds quicker than

216
00:08:30,852 --> 00:08:31,175
that.

217
00:08:31,355 --> 00:08:33,421
In total, from first to last,

218
00:08:33,421 --> 00:08:35,843
the car found three point one four

219
00:08:35,843 --> 00:08:38,764
seconds, which is about three point seven

220
00:08:38,764 --> 00:08:40,759
percent of the original lap.

221
00:08:41,108 --> 00:08:42,699
Who held the record before?

222
00:08:42,879 --> 00:08:45,906
Both Backfire News and FastCar say the

223
00:08:45,906 --> 00:08:49,649
McLaren Senna, at one minute twenty-seven point

224
00:08:49,649 --> 00:08:50,286
six two.

225
00:08:50,466 --> 00:08:52,335
A McLaren press release,

226
00:08:52,335 --> 00:08:55,762
reported by duPont Registry in August twenty

227
00:08:55,762 --> 00:08:59,189
twenty, says a stock twenty twenty-one Senna

228
00:08:59,189 --> 00:09:01,370
set that time and beat a one

229
00:09:01,370 --> 00:09:04,874
twenty-eight record that had stood for years.

230
00:09:05,054 --> 00:09:07,685
So the 21C's first lap beat the

231
00:09:07,685 --> 00:09:09,977
Senna by about two seconds.

232
00:09:10,326 --> 00:09:11,638
By our arithmetic,

233
00:09:11,638 --> 00:09:14,771
the December twenty twenty-five lap is five

234
00:09:14,771 --> 00:09:17,685
point three two seconds quicker than the

235
00:09:17,685 --> 00:09:20,381
Senna's, about six point one percent.

236
00:09:20,561 --> 00:09:23,373
Those are different cars and different years,

237
00:09:23,373 --> 00:09:25,497
and the sources do not say whether

238
00:09:25,497 --> 00:09:26,935
the conditions matched.

239
00:09:27,115 --> 00:09:29,145
But as a ladder of records at

240
00:09:29,145 --> 00:09:30,335
one famous track,

241
00:09:30,335 --> 00:09:31,666
it is hard to miss.

242
00:09:32,015 --> 00:09:33,995
The track is not the only place

243
00:09:33,995 --> 00:09:35,464
the car has been timed.

244
00:09:35,644 --> 00:09:39,116
Wikipedia lists a Goodwood Festival of Speed

245
00:09:39,116 --> 00:09:42,666
hillclimb time of forty-eight point eight two

246
00:09:42,666 --> 00:09:45,744
seconds, driven by Chris Ward in twenty

247
00:09:45,744 --> 00:09:49,058
twenty-four, and a Circuit of the Americas

248
00:09:49,058 --> 00:09:51,741
lap of two minutes ten point seven

249
00:09:51,741 --> 00:09:53,477
in twenty twenty-four.

250
00:09:53,657 --> 00:09:55,728
It does not name the driver for

251
00:09:55,728 --> 00:09:56,664
the Texas lap.

252
00:09:57,013 --> 00:10:00,531
Wikipedia calls these production-car records.

253
00:10:00,711 --> 00:10:01,988
That wording matters.

254
00:10:02,168 --> 00:10:05,407
A production-car record is not an absolute

255
00:10:05,407 --> 00:10:07,489
lap record for any vehicle,

256
00:10:07,489 --> 00:10:10,341
and the rules behind these claims are

257
00:10:10,341 --> 00:10:12,269
not given in our sources.

258
00:10:12,449 --> 00:10:14,587
Backfire's article, for example,

259
00:10:14,587 --> 00:10:16,592
does not say who timed the lap

260
00:10:16,592 --> 00:10:19,264
or whether it was officially sanctioned.

261
00:10:19,444 --> 00:10:21,546
So we call them reported records.

262
00:10:21,895 --> 00:10:23,382
We promised two things,

263
00:10:23,382 --> 00:10:24,740
but there is a third.

264
00:10:24,920 --> 00:10:27,533
The car's own numbers do not agree

265
00:10:27,533 --> 00:10:28,763
between sources.

266
00:10:28,943 --> 00:10:29,906
Take power.

267
00:10:30,086 --> 00:10:33,198
Wikipedia says twelve hundred fifty horsepower.

268
00:10:33,378 --> 00:10:37,081
GTPlanet and FastCar say twelve thirty-three.

269
00:10:37,261 --> 00:10:40,106
Backfire News says thirteen thirty-one.

270
00:10:40,286 --> 00:10:43,922
Czinger's special Blackbird edition is listed at

271
00:10:43,922 --> 00:10:45,058
thirteen fifty.

272
00:10:45,238 --> 00:10:48,303
Four figures for one family of cars.

273
00:10:48,652 --> 00:10:49,895
Weight is worse.

274
00:10:50,075 --> 00:10:53,297
Wikipedia gives a curb weight of sixteen

275
00:10:53,297 --> 00:10:55,311
hundred twenty kilograms.

276
00:10:55,491 --> 00:10:58,530
GTPlanet in twenty twenty says about twelve

277
00:10:58,530 --> 00:10:59,520
hundred fifty.

278
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:02,314
FastCar says twelve hundred forty,

279
00:11:02,314 --> 00:11:03,775
claimed by Czinger.

280
00:11:03,955 --> 00:11:07,356
Backfire says under twenty-eight hundred pounds.

281
00:11:07,536 --> 00:11:08,842
By our arithmetic,

282
00:11:08,842 --> 00:11:11,743
that is a three hundred seventy kilogram

283
00:11:11,743 --> 00:11:13,629
gap between the first two,

284
00:11:13,629 --> 00:11:15,152
about thirty percent.

285
00:11:15,502 --> 00:11:17,047
Top speed too.

286
00:11:17,227 --> 00:11:20,091
GTPlanet's body text says a claimed two

287
00:11:20,091 --> 00:11:22,589
hundred sixty-eight miles an hour,

288
00:11:22,589 --> 00:11:25,087
and the headline says two seventy.

289
00:11:25,267 --> 00:11:28,285
ASME's video caption says two sixty-eight.

290
00:11:28,465 --> 00:11:32,320
FastCar says Czinger claims two hundred eighty-one.

291
00:11:32,500 --> 00:11:35,395
Even the acceleration claims differ:

292
00:11:35,395 --> 00:11:37,567
a zero to sixty time of one

293
00:11:37,567 --> 00:11:40,543
point eight seconds in one source and

294
00:11:40,543 --> 00:11:43,680
one point nine to sixty-two in another.

295
00:11:44,030 --> 00:11:45,598
Why do the numbers move?

296
00:11:45,778 --> 00:11:47,392
The sources do not say.

297
00:11:47,572 --> 00:11:50,466
The earlier ones are from twenty twenty,

298
00:11:50,466 --> 00:11:52,854
when the car was still a promise.

299
00:11:53,034 --> 00:11:55,414
The later ones come after development.

300
00:11:55,594 --> 00:11:57,452
Our reading, and it is only a

301
00:11:57,452 --> 00:12:00,207
reading, is that early figures were targets

302
00:12:00,207 --> 00:12:02,769
and later ones are closer to production.

303
00:12:02,949 --> 00:12:04,572
No source states that,

304
00:12:04,572 --> 00:12:07,007
so we show every number and trust

305
00:12:07,007 --> 00:12:08,557
none of them blindly.

306
00:12:08,906 --> 00:12:11,446
The most useful reading for a viewer

307
00:12:11,446 --> 00:12:13,703
is this: treat the lap times and

308
00:12:13,703 --> 00:12:17,654
the printed-parts claims as the better-documented facts,

309
00:12:17,654 --> 00:12:19,911
because they come with a driver,

310
00:12:19,911 --> 00:12:21,886
a track and a named article.

311
00:12:22,066 --> 00:12:23,561
Treat top speed,

312
00:12:23,561 --> 00:12:26,083
weight and power as claims.

313
00:12:26,263 --> 00:12:29,780
Wikipedia itself has two conflicts inside its

314
00:12:29,780 --> 00:12:32,672
own page, on the peak-power rev limit

315
00:12:32,672 --> 00:12:35,564
and on how many electric motors there

316
00:12:35,564 --> 00:12:35,877
are.

317
00:12:36,226 --> 00:12:37,925
There is a good reason to care

318
00:12:37,925 --> 00:12:39,001
about those motors.

319
00:12:39,181 --> 00:12:42,145
Wikipedia says the car has one axial-flux

320
00:12:42,145 --> 00:12:45,614
motor generator unit plus two high-output motors

321
00:12:45,614 --> 00:12:47,204
with torque vectoring.

322
00:12:47,384 --> 00:12:49,456
The text says two motors on the

323
00:12:49,456 --> 00:12:49,857
front.

324
00:12:50,037 --> 00:12:53,780
The battery is a two kilowatt-hour lithium-titanate

325
00:12:53,780 --> 00:12:54,147
pack.

326
00:12:54,327 --> 00:12:58,490
GTPlanet says that chemistry charges and discharges

327
00:12:58,490 --> 00:13:02,326
faster than lithium-ion but stores less energy.

328
00:13:02,675 --> 00:13:05,456
Put together: a two point eight eight

329
00:13:05,456 --> 00:13:08,161
litre V8 that Wikipedia lists with a

330
00:13:08,161 --> 00:13:11,468
bore of eighty-four millimetres and a stroke

331
00:13:11,468 --> 00:13:15,075
of sixty-five, a seven-speed sequential gearbox,

332
00:13:15,075 --> 00:13:17,931
and electric motors that fill in while

333
00:13:17,931 --> 00:13:19,208
the turbos spool.

334
00:13:19,388 --> 00:13:20,655
By our arithmetic,

335
00:13:20,655 --> 00:13:23,470
those bore and stroke figures give about

336
00:13:23,470 --> 00:13:27,129
two thousand eight hundred eighty cubic centimetres,

337
00:13:27,129 --> 00:13:29,944
which matches the stated two point eight

338
00:13:29,944 --> 00:13:30,859
eight litres.

339
00:13:31,208 --> 00:13:32,477
A word on timing,

340
00:13:32,477 --> 00:13:34,715
because it shapes the numbers.

341
00:13:34,895 --> 00:13:37,915
Wikipedia says the 21C was unveiled on

342
00:13:37,915 --> 00:13:40,935
the eleventh of March twenty twenty in

343
00:13:40,935 --> 00:13:44,035
London, after the Geneva Motor Show was

344
00:13:44,035 --> 00:13:46,737
cancelled because of the pandemic.

345
00:13:46,917 --> 00:13:49,995
Deliveries were planned for the fourth quarter

346
00:13:49,995 --> 00:13:51,535
of twenty twenty-three,

347
00:13:51,535 --> 00:13:53,676
with most in twenty twenty-four,

348
00:13:53,676 --> 00:13:55,349
according to the founder,

349
00:13:55,349 --> 00:13:56,286
Kevin Czinger.

350
00:13:56,636 --> 00:14:00,055
The Backfire article of March twenty twenty-four

351
00:14:00,055 --> 00:14:02,903
says production was expected to start in

352
00:14:02,903 --> 00:14:05,965
twenty twenty-two, and that a chip shortage

353
00:14:05,965 --> 00:14:06,749
delayed it.

354
00:14:06,929 --> 00:14:09,094
It also says three more models were

355
00:14:09,094 --> 00:14:10,888
planned, one of them cheaper.

356
00:14:11,068 --> 00:14:13,114
We found no source in this set

357
00:14:13,114 --> 00:14:16,048
confirming how many cars have actually been

358
00:14:16,048 --> 00:14:18,504
delivered, so we cannot say how many

359
00:14:18,504 --> 00:14:19,868
of the eighty exist.

360
00:14:20,218 --> 00:14:22,552
The Blackbird is the rarest variant.

361
00:14:22,732 --> 00:14:25,270
Wikipedia says it is limited to four

362
00:14:25,270 --> 00:14:28,653
examples with thirteen hundred fifty horsepower,

363
00:14:28,653 --> 00:14:30,627
against the standard eighty.

364
00:14:30,807 --> 00:14:32,167
By our arithmetic,

365
00:14:32,167 --> 00:14:34,812
that is five percent of the planned

366
00:14:34,812 --> 00:14:35,114
run.

367
00:14:35,294 --> 00:14:37,324
The sources give no price and no

368
00:14:37,324 --> 00:14:40,368
performance figures for the Blackbird beyond the

369
00:14:40,368 --> 00:14:41,192
power number.

370
00:14:41,541 --> 00:14:44,142
Start with how the power is split.

371
00:14:44,322 --> 00:14:47,215
Facfox says the twelve hundred fifty total

372
00:14:47,215 --> 00:14:49,695
is about nine hundred fifty from the

373
00:14:49,695 --> 00:14:52,450
twin-turbo V8 and three hundred from the

374
00:14:52,450 --> 00:14:53,552
electric system.

375
00:14:53,732 --> 00:14:56,474
GTPlanet says the V8 makes over nine

376
00:14:56,474 --> 00:14:59,216
hundred thirty and each of two front

377
00:14:59,216 --> 00:15:01,882
motors adds one hundred sixty-five.

378
00:15:02,062 --> 00:15:03,561
By our arithmetic,

379
00:15:03,561 --> 00:15:06,975
nine thirty plus two times one sixty-five

380
00:15:06,975 --> 00:15:08,307
is twelve sixty,

381
00:15:08,307 --> 00:15:10,305
not twelve thirty-three.

382
00:15:10,655 --> 00:15:13,539
The sources do not explain that twenty-seven

383
00:15:13,539 --> 00:15:14,522
horsepower gap.

384
00:15:14,702 --> 00:15:16,832
Our reading is that the engine and

385
00:15:16,832 --> 00:15:19,025
motors do not both reach their peak

386
00:15:19,025 --> 00:15:20,216
at the same moment,

387
00:15:20,216 --> 00:15:22,283
so the quoted total is lower than

388
00:15:22,283 --> 00:15:23,223
the simple sum.

389
00:15:23,403 --> 00:15:25,967
That is a common pattern in hybrids,

390
00:15:25,967 --> 00:15:28,175
but none of our sources says it

391
00:15:28,175 --> 00:15:29,243
about this car,

392
00:15:29,243 --> 00:15:31,379
so treat it as our hypothesis.

393
00:15:31,729 --> 00:15:34,748
The engine's speed is remarkable on paper.

394
00:15:34,928 --> 00:15:38,384
GTPlanet and FastCar both give an eleven

395
00:15:38,384 --> 00:15:40,199
thousand rpm redline.

396
00:15:40,379 --> 00:15:43,477
With the stroke of sixty-five millimetres that

397
00:15:43,477 --> 00:15:46,441
Wikipedia lists, our arithmetic gives a mean

398
00:15:46,441 --> 00:15:49,404
piston speed of about twenty-four metres per

399
00:15:49,404 --> 00:15:50,886
second at the redline.

400
00:15:51,066 --> 00:15:53,490
We have no comparison engine in our

401
00:15:53,490 --> 00:15:56,190
sources, so we offer the number without

402
00:15:56,190 --> 00:15:56,883
a verdict.

403
00:15:57,232 --> 00:15:59,798
Aerodynamics is another conflict.

404
00:15:59,978 --> 00:16:02,765
GTPlanet says the road car makes five

405
00:16:02,765 --> 00:16:05,777
hundred fifty pounds of downforce at one

406
00:16:05,777 --> 00:16:08,263
hundred fifty-five miles an hour,

407
00:16:08,263 --> 00:16:11,577
and the track model seventeen hundred forty.

408
00:16:11,757 --> 00:16:15,277
FastCar says twenty-five hundred kilograms at two

409
00:16:15,277 --> 00:16:17,648
hundred miles an hour in the most

410
00:16:17,648 --> 00:16:19,013
aggressive setting.

411
00:16:19,193 --> 00:16:20,528
By our arithmetic,

412
00:16:20,528 --> 00:16:23,346
five hundred fifty pounds is about two

413
00:16:23,346 --> 00:16:25,126
hundred fifty kilograms.

414
00:16:25,475 --> 00:16:28,040
The track version is not very different

415
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:30,210
in weight, according to GTPlanet,

416
00:16:30,210 --> 00:16:32,577
which says it is only seventy pounds

417
00:16:32,577 --> 00:16:33,103
lighter.

418
00:16:33,283 --> 00:16:34,628
By our arithmetic,

419
00:16:34,628 --> 00:16:37,243
that is about thirty-two kilograms,

420
00:16:37,243 --> 00:16:39,708
roughly two and a half percent of

421
00:16:39,708 --> 00:16:42,397
a twelve hundred fifty kilogram car.

422
00:16:42,577 --> 00:16:44,440
If that figure is right,

423
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:47,934
the track model's advantage comes mostly from

424
00:16:47,934 --> 00:16:49,720
aerodynamics and setup,

425
00:16:49,720 --> 00:16:51,505
not from shedding mass.

426
00:16:51,855 --> 00:16:55,965
Acceleration claims also come with different distances.

427
00:16:56,145 --> 00:16:59,260
FastCar quotes Czinger claims of an eight

428
00:16:59,260 --> 00:17:01,540
point one second quarter mile,

429
00:17:01,540 --> 00:17:04,503
zero to one hundred eighty-six miles an

430
00:17:04,503 --> 00:17:07,010
hour in eight point five seconds,

431
00:17:07,010 --> 00:17:09,897
and zero to two hundred forty-eight in

432
00:17:09,897 --> 00:17:11,645
twenty-seven point one.

433
00:17:11,825 --> 00:17:15,010
GTPlanet says zero to two forty-nine in

434
00:17:15,010 --> 00:17:16,643
twenty-nine seconds.

435
00:17:16,823 --> 00:17:18,187
By our arithmetic,

436
00:17:18,187 --> 00:17:20,991
that is one point nine seconds apart.

437
00:17:21,340 --> 00:17:24,727
Wikipedia's infobox has two more figures with

438
00:17:24,727 --> 00:17:28,189
no context: seven hundred fifty horsepower and

439
00:17:28,189 --> 00:17:31,801
three hundred ninety-seven pound-feet of torque.

440
00:17:31,981 --> 00:17:34,415
The page does not say whether those

441
00:17:34,415 --> 00:17:36,222
describe the engine alone,

442
00:17:36,222 --> 00:17:38,239
one motor, or something else.

443
00:17:38,419 --> 00:17:40,730
Seven fifty is not twelve fifty,

444
00:17:40,730 --> 00:17:43,114
so it clearly is not the combined

445
00:17:43,114 --> 00:17:43,620
number.

446
00:17:43,800 --> 00:17:45,584
We could not match it to any

447
00:17:45,584 --> 00:17:46,412
other source.

448
00:17:46,762 --> 00:17:49,433
Even the gearbox has two descriptions.

449
00:17:49,613 --> 00:17:54,119
Wikipedia says a seven-speed sequential transaxle with

450
00:17:54,119 --> 00:17:56,706
a hydraulic multi-plate clutch.

451
00:17:56,886 --> 00:18:01,136
ASME says an automated seven-speed semi-sequential unit.

452
00:18:01,316 --> 00:18:04,086
Those may be the same hardware described

453
00:18:04,086 --> 00:18:05,402
in different words,

454
00:18:05,402 --> 00:18:07,411
or they may differ in detail.

455
00:18:07,591 --> 00:18:09,595
The sources do not settle it,

456
00:18:09,595 --> 00:18:11,738
but both agree on seven speeds,

457
00:18:11,738 --> 00:18:13,742
and on the fact that the case

458
00:18:13,742 --> 00:18:14,986
itself is printed.

459
00:18:15,336 --> 00:18:17,600
One more number from ASME is about

460
00:18:17,600 --> 00:18:18,332
the future.

461
00:18:18,512 --> 00:18:21,386
The article says Divergent has existed for

462
00:18:21,386 --> 00:18:22,686
eight to ten years,

463
00:18:22,686 --> 00:18:25,697
with a mission of toolless manufacturing for

464
00:18:25,697 --> 00:18:27,544
primary vehicle structures.

465
00:18:27,724 --> 00:18:29,383
It lists next steps:

466
00:18:29,383 --> 00:18:33,944
integrating cooling, electrical and fluid networks into

467
00:18:33,944 --> 00:18:37,012
structural parts, scaling production,

468
00:18:37,012 --> 00:18:41,408
reducing cost and possibly printing replacement parts

469
00:18:41,408 --> 00:18:42,237
on demand.

470
00:18:42,417 --> 00:18:43,660
Those are plans,

471
00:18:43,660 --> 00:18:45,447
not delivered features.

472
00:18:45,797 --> 00:18:48,427
We left out things that sound plausible

473
00:18:48,427 --> 00:18:50,450
but that no source in this set

474
00:18:50,450 --> 00:18:51,057
supports.

475
00:18:51,237 --> 00:18:53,872
How many printed parts the car has.

476
00:18:54,052 --> 00:18:55,980
How much weight the printing saves.

477
00:18:56,160 --> 00:18:58,052
What each part costs.

478
00:18:58,232 --> 00:19:00,456
Whether the car handles better than a

479
00:19:00,456 --> 00:19:01,959
conventionally built one.

480
00:19:02,139 --> 00:19:03,935
Every one of those is a fair

481
00:19:03,935 --> 00:19:06,374
question, and none is answered in what

482
00:19:06,374 --> 00:19:06,887
we read.

483
00:19:07,237 --> 00:19:08,561
There is also the name.

484
00:19:08,741 --> 00:19:11,364
We found no source here that explains

485
00:19:11,364 --> 00:19:13,420
what twenty-one C stands for.

486
00:19:13,600 --> 00:19:15,939
Many people assume it refers to the

487
00:19:15,939 --> 00:19:18,746
twenty-first century, but that is a guess,

488
00:19:18,746 --> 00:19:20,752
not a fact from these sources.

489
00:19:20,932 --> 00:19:23,471
When a name has no stated meaning,

490
00:19:23,471 --> 00:19:26,010
we would rather say so than invent

491
00:19:26,010 --> 00:19:26,980
a tidy story.

492
00:19:27,330 --> 00:19:29,294
So here is the honest summary of

493
00:19:29,294 --> 00:19:29,908
thing one.

494
00:19:30,088 --> 00:19:33,353
The strongest documented fact is the toolless

495
00:19:33,353 --> 00:19:36,691
construction approach, with three named parts:

496
00:19:36,691 --> 00:19:37,925
the gearbox case,

497
00:19:37,925 --> 00:19:40,537
the rear subframe and the BrakeNode.

498
00:19:40,717 --> 00:19:43,116
The strongest quantity is the three to

499
00:19:43,116 --> 00:19:45,326
four thousand labour hours per car.

500
00:19:45,506 --> 00:19:47,727
Everything else about the build,

501
00:19:47,727 --> 00:19:49,948
such as savings and part counts,

502
00:19:49,948 --> 00:19:51,961
is either a claim or missing.

503
00:19:52,311 --> 00:19:54,111
And the summary of thing two.

504
00:19:54,291 --> 00:19:57,678
The strongest documented fact is the sequence

505
00:19:57,678 --> 00:19:59,258
of Laguna Seca times:

506
00:19:59,258 --> 00:20:01,290
one twenty-five forty-four,

507
00:20:01,290 --> 00:20:03,473
one twenty-four seventy-five,

508
00:20:03,473 --> 00:20:05,129
one twenty-two thirty,

509
00:20:05,129 --> 00:20:08,892
with the Senna's one twenty-seven sixty-two before

510
00:20:08,892 --> 00:20:09,268
them.

511
00:20:09,448 --> 00:20:11,684
The weakest part is who certified the

512
00:20:11,684 --> 00:20:13,860
times, and what the conditions were.

513
00:20:14,040 --> 00:20:17,000
Those details would make the claim airtight,

514
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:18,614
and we do not have them.

515
00:20:18,963 --> 00:20:21,262
Why do these two things belong together?

516
00:20:21,442 --> 00:20:23,439
Our reading is that the lap times

517
00:20:23,439 --> 00:20:25,738
are the proof the printed structure is

518
00:20:25,738 --> 00:20:26,585
not a gimmick.

519
00:20:26,765 --> 00:20:28,695
If a car built this way can

520
00:20:28,695 --> 00:20:31,267
lap a famous circuit faster than the

521
00:20:31,267 --> 00:20:33,553
best McLaren hypercar before it,

522
00:20:33,553 --> 00:20:36,053
then the method deserves attention.

523
00:20:36,233 --> 00:20:37,839
But that is our argument,

524
00:20:37,839 --> 00:20:40,216
not Czinger's, and the sources do not

525
00:20:40,216 --> 00:20:41,179
make it for us.

526
00:20:41,529 --> 00:20:44,081
If you want to check any of

527
00:20:44,081 --> 00:20:47,390
this yourself, the links are in the

528
00:20:47,390 --> 00:20:50,414
description: the Wikipedia page,

529
00:20:50,414 --> 00:20:52,021
the ASME article,

530
00:20:52,021 --> 00:20:54,384
the Backfire News report,

531
00:20:54,384 --> 00:20:57,598
GTPlanet, FastCar, Facfox and CSQ.

532
00:20:57,778 --> 00:21:00,238
Read the weight and speed numbers with

533
00:21:00,238 --> 00:21:01,663
the conflicts in mind,

534
00:21:01,663 --> 00:21:03,541
and look for the date of each

535
00:21:03,541 --> 00:21:05,742
article before you trust a figure.

536
00:21:06,092 --> 00:21:08,835
That is the Czinger twenty-one C:

537
00:21:08,835 --> 00:21:12,159
a hypercar built mostly without tooling,

538
00:21:12,159 --> 00:21:14,985
and a record holder at Laguna Seca

539
00:21:14,985 --> 00:21:17,644
whose lap time fell three times.

540
00:21:17,824 --> 00:21:19,156
If you enjoyed it,

541
00:21:19,156 --> 00:21:21,226
subscribe to Pickup Reality.

542
00:21:21,406 --> 00:21:23,968
The next video puts the car against

543
00:21:23,968 --> 00:21:25,285
the McLaren Senna,

544
00:21:25,285 --> 00:21:26,237
head to head,

545
00:21:26,237 --> 00:21:28,140
with every number sourced.

546
00:21:28,489 --> 00:21:30,428
One last reminder on method.

547
00:21:30,608 --> 00:21:32,969
Every number in this video came from

548
00:21:32,969 --> 00:21:33,952
a named source.

549
00:21:34,132 --> 00:21:36,711
Where we calculated something ourselves,

550
00:21:36,711 --> 00:21:37,743
it was labelled.

551
00:21:37,923 --> 00:21:40,108
Where two sources disagreed,

552
00:21:40,108 --> 00:21:41,278
we showed both.

553
00:21:41,458 --> 00:21:43,458
Where nothing was available,

554
00:21:43,458 --> 00:21:44,244
we said so.

555
00:21:44,424 --> 00:21:46,147
That is how we try to treat

556
00:21:46,147 --> 00:21:47,805
every car on this channel,

557
00:21:47,805 --> 00:21:49,400
and it is why we show the

558
00:21:49,400 --> 00:21:50,357
gaps on screen.
